So next we move on to the main part of the morning program, which is to ask the question of how AI might change the nature of
discovery and to introduce that section of the programme.

136
00:15:42,702 --> 00:15:44,410
我们将听取 Alison Noble 的简短演讲。
We have a short talk by Alison Noble.

137
00:15:44,530 --> 00:15:56,320
Alison 是 University of Oxford 的生物医学工程教授，她还曾担任 Royal Society of the UK
关于人工智能时代科学的专项工作组主席，该小组在
Alison is Professor of Biomedical Engineering at the University of Oxford, and she also chaired the Royal Society of the UK's working
group on science in the age of AI, which produced a report in 2024 and is going to produce another report in 2027.

138
00:15:56,320 --> 00:16:01,010
2024年发布了一份报告，并计划在2027年再发布一份。
Alison is Professor of Biomedical Engineering at the University of Oxford, and she also chaired the Royal Society of the UK's working
group on science in the age of AI, which produced a report in 2024 and is going to produce another report in 2027.

139
00:16:01,150 --> 00:16:05,924
我相信，她将简要总结这些报告的发现以及它们带给我们的启示。
I believe, and she's going to summarise briefly the findings of those reports and where it's taking us.

140
00:16:06,090 --> 00:16:07,250
请欢迎 Alison Noble。
Please welcome Alison Noble.

141
00:16:14,580 --> 00:16:29,700
很高兴能来到这里和大家聊聊 Royal Society 的工作及其部分研究结果，
这些内容特别侧重于科学进程的演变、科学家的职责以及这些变化在整个科学领域的体现。
I'm delighted to be here to talk to you a little bit about the Royal Society's work and some of its findings and it was focused
particularly on how the scientific process is evolving and what a scientist does and how that's changing across science.

142
00:16:30,574 --> 00:16:44,300
第一份报告于2024年发布，它基于与众多科学家的多次讨论和圆桌会议，
特别是探讨了当时科学家认为最重要的核心问题。
The first report was published in 2024, it followed many discussions with scientists getting them together in round tables and
particularly the question was, what do scientists say mattered at that time?

143
00:16:45,102 --> 00:16:56,324
主要的担忧在于数据获取、对海量数据的需求，以及高质量代表性数据整理的重要性。
The big concerns were about data access, requiring the requirements for large volumes and quantities of data,
and the importance of curation of well-representative data.

144
00:16:56,880 --> 00:17:02,860
当然，对于我们中的许多人来说，这也是一个国际性的问题：我们如何共同协作处理数据？
And of course, that's an international question for many of us, is how can we work together with data?

145
00:17:04,240 --> 00:17:06,560
计算基础设施也极具挑战性。
Computational infrastructure is also very challenging.

146
00:17:07,319 --> 00:17:21,640
传统上，科学是基于个人办公桌的，电脑就在你的桌子上；
而现在则需要区域和国家层面的基础设施投资，以便你有足够的算力来研究人工智能。
Traditionally, science being based on the desk, with your computer on your desk, moving to now require regional and national
investment in infrastructures so that you can have the compute capacity to work on AI.

147
00:17:23,246 --> 00:17:26,358
跨学科性的角色也在发生变化。
The roles of interdisciplinarity are also changing.

148
00:17:26,766 --> 00:17:38,040
这意味着科学领域如何协同工作，比如计算机科学家与
临床医学的结合（就像我的情况），以及打破学科壁垒。
That means how science disciplines work together, computer scientists with
clinical medicine, as in my case, but also going across discipline barriers.

149
00:17:38,540 --> 00:17:44,964
这也意味着你要从孤立的领域走向团队合作，因此团队科学的重要性日益凸显。
And that means you're going from siloed areas, starting to work in teams, so the importance of team science.

150
00:17:45,380 --> 00:17:50,380
在科学领域，这带来了巨大的挑战，因为竞争驱动了我们很多行为。
And in science, this creates big challenges where competition drives a lot of what we do.

151
00:17:50,766 --> 00:17:56,708
而合作伙伴关系可能会推动未来的发展，那么我们该如何建立奖励机制来管理这种模式呢？
And the notion that partnership might drive some of the future, how do we set up reward systems to manage that?

152
00:17:58,340 --> 00:18:01,620
可重复性一直是科学的重要基石。
Reproducibility has always been a great foundation of science.

153
00:18:01,620 --> 00:18:04,900
你需要能够让其他人复现你的结果。
you need to be able to reproduce your results to others.

154
00:18:05,390 --> 00:18:07,300
当我们面对黑盒时，这就变得很有挑战性。
This is challenging when we have black boxes.

155
00:18:07,980 --> 00:18:19,364
这属于我们所说的负责任的AI，也就是如果你得出了结果，就必须能够复现它，管理数据偏差，
并解释结果中必须具备的可解释性。
And this falls under what we call responsible AI, that you need to, if you produce a result, be able to reproduce it,
manage data bias, explain the explainability that must come with results.

156
00:18:19,960 --> 00:18:25,764
而且还要能以开放的方式提供你的模型和数据，也就是开放性和透明度。
And also be able to offer your models and data in an open way, so openness and transparency.

157
00:18:26,360 --> 00:18:29,860
这些在人工智能领域，比以前面临的挑战要大得多。
These are, again, with AI, are much bigger challenges than before.

158
00:18:29,860 --> 00:18:36,500
这非常重要，这样我们产生的信息和知识才让人信赖。
And this is really important so that we generate evidence and knowledge that people can trust.

159
00:18:38,062 --> 00:18:42,700
关于算法，结论是它们会不断演变，我们也看到了这一点。
And on algorithms, the conclusion was they will just evolve, and we've seen that.

160
00:18:42,800 --> 00:18:51,580
我们看到了卷积神经网络、Transformer架构、大语言模型，以及最近出现的智能体AI和世界模型。
We've seen convolutional neural networks, transformers, large language
models, and more recently, agentic AI and world models come along.

161
00:18:51,840 --> 00:18:54,200
科学家们正在吸收所有这些新技术。
And scientists are absorbing all these new technologies.

162
00:18:54,200 --> 00:18:59,580
但我们必须记住，虽然我们用人工智能这个词，但它并不是单一的东西。
But we must remember that we use the word AI but it's not one thing.

163
00:19:00,100 --> 00:19:05,796
我们可以把人工智能作为一种强大的能力来辅助发现，利用它进行模式识别和预测的能力。
We can have AI as a superpower for discovery with its ability to do pattern recognition predictions.

164
00:19:06,542 --> 00:19:10,160
我们也可以把人工智能作为你的助手，来赋能个人。
We can have AI as your assistant to empower an individual.

165
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:15,320
所以，你的研究助理在支持你的研究工作，这是一个新概念。
So your research assistant supporting your research assistants is a new concept.

166
00:19:15,940 --> 00:19:21,240
人工智能为你完成编程，让你有更多时间去思考和思考。
The AI do the coding for you, allows you to do more reflection and thought yourself.

167
00:19:21,240 --> 00:19:31,920
而且我们看到它在整个科学领域的广泛应用，从生物、医学
到计算机视觉、材料、化学，还有像气候建模之类的领域。
and the diverse applications we're seeing across the whole of science from biology,
medicine to computer vision, materials, chemistry and things like climate modeling.

168
00:19:32,846 --> 00:19:40,520
接下来谈到2026年，我们正在更新报告，我想先提一下一些初步发现。
So moving on to 2026, we're doing an update of our report and I just thought I'd mention some early findings.

169
00:19:41,518 --> 00:19:45,080
首先，应用变得更深了，而不是更广了。
First of all, applications have got deeper rather than broader.

170
00:19:45,966 --> 00:19:57,620
不同的学科有不同的体验，例如数学家，正如我们之前听到的，他们认为人工智能很有创意，
但对他们作为一个学科的身份构成了挑战。
Some of the different disciplines are having different experiences, for example, mathematicians, for example, as we've heard earlier,
they're concluding that AI is really creative but challenging who they are as a discipline.

171
00:19:58,260 --> 00:20:04,740
其他领域，比如气候科学家，已经在使用人工智能，并且可能也在了解它的局限性。
Other areas such as climate scientists have used the AI and maybe understanding some of its limitations as well.

172
00:20:05,262 --> 00:20:10,404
所以我们看到随着技术的普及，出现了不同的体验。
So we're seeing this as the technology diffuses in, we're seeing different experiences.

173
00:20:11,726 --> 00:20:16,920
我们还有一些新事物，比如基于人工智能的科学，
其中包含很多在搞清楚原理之前就能奏效的东西。
We're also got new things like AI-based sciences full of things that work before we know why.

174
00:20:16,920 --> 00:20:19,880
那么我们要发表这些研究，然后再去弄清楚原因吗？
So do we publish these and figure out why later?

175
00:20:20,238 --> 00:20:25,796
所以出现了一种新类型的科学出版物，我们需要仔细思考如何管理它。
So there's a new type of science publication and we need to think carefully how we manage that.

176
00:20:26,880 --> 00:20:30,000
不过，可复现性仍然是我们的挑战之一。
Reproducibility though remains one of our challenges.

177
00:20:31,726 --> 00:20:43,880
还有使用大语言模型，它们是围绕序列和语言构建的，
输出并不总是反映现实或我们感兴趣的科学问题。
And also using large language models, they're built around sequences and languages and the outputs do not always reflect reality or
the scientific questions we're interested in.

178
00:20:44,206 --> 00:20:46,420
所以我们需要谨慎处理这个问题。
so we need to be careful how we manage that.

179
00:20:47,502 --> 00:20:58,948
这引出了人的因素，我们需要在科学领域更深入地了解我们如何信任结果，
比如认知过载和过度依赖等问题。
And this brings on to the human factors and we need to develop deeper understanding in science of how we trust results,
things like cognitive overloading and over-reliance.

180
00:20:59,534 --> 00:21:04,580
我们特别需要思考在科学背景下的判断力和接受建议的问题。
We need to particularly think about judgment and advice-taking in the context of science.

181
00:21:05,220 --> 00:21:16,676
例如，让人工智能查询你的结果并提供证据来验证假设是没问题的，但你绝不能过度依赖它。
For example, it's fine to ask AI to query your results and the evidence
to confirm a hypothesis, but you mustn't become over-reliant on it.

182
00:21:17,486 --> 00:21:22,720
最后，我们面临所谓的学徒问题，这是一个普遍存在的焦虑。
And finally, we have what we call the apprentice problem, and that's a shared anxiety.

183
00:21:23,220 --> 00:21:35,716
如果团队角色在变化，而人工智能正在承担科学家过去花费大量时间去做的任务，
那么对于未来专家的职业路径就会产生很多不确定性。
There is a lot of uncertainty about career paths of future experts if team roles are
changing and AI is doing tasks that scientists used to spend time doing in the past.

184
00:21:35,740 --> 00:21:37,900
所以，人工智能是一个工具；
So, AI is a tool;

185
00:21:38,030 --> 00:21:49,600
它是一个极其强大的工具，但我们必须确保科学和科学家不会因此变得缺乏创造力，
或者在处理不符合趋势的问题时能力下降。
it's an incredibly powerful tool, but we need to ensure science, and scientists do not become less creative and are not less able to
tackle the problems that do not follow trends.

186
00:21:49,902 --> 00:21:52,940
而且我们不能盲目追随潮流。
and we mustn't follow trends.

187
00:21:53,198 --> 00:22:06,596
也不能过度依赖主流方法，我们需要不断创造机会去发现那些意想不到的科学突破，
这些突破几个世纪以来一直是科学进步的基石。
and the dominant approaches too much, and we need to create, continue to create the opportunities for the unexpected scientific
discoveries which have for centuries been the foundation of scientific advancement.

